<BaseLayout title="About Mossy Tank" description="What Mossy Tank is, how our guides are made, and what you can expect from the advice here.">
  <div class="container prose post">
    <h1>About Mossy Tank</h1>

    <p>
      Mossy Tank is a research-led guide to planted aquariums — built specifically for beginners
      who want a thriving, low-maintenance tank without the overwhelm that usually comes with it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The aquascaping hobby has an information problem. The advice is scattered across decade-old
      forums, contradictory YouTube comments, and product pages with obvious incentives to oversell
      complexity. Beginners either get buried in jargon or make expensive, avoidable mistakes —
      often both.
    </p>

    <p>
      We built Mossy Tank to fix that. Every guide here is researched from the ground up: drawing
      on established hobbyist communities, peer-reviewed aquatic science, manufacturer data, and
      the collective knowledge of experienced aquascapers. We write for the person who just wants
      to know what to actually do — not every theoretical possibility.
    </p>

    <h2>How we approach our content</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Beginner-first.</strong> We explain every concept from first principles. If you need to already know something to understand our advice, we've failed.</li>
      <li><strong>Research-backed.</strong> Recommendations are based on community consensus and verified sources — not product marketing or guesswork.</li>
      <li><strong>Honest about uncertainty.</strong> Where experienced hobbyists disagree, we say so. There are no "only one right answer" declarations on contested topics.</li>
      <li><strong>Low-tech focus.</strong> We believe most beginners are better served by simple, forgiving setups. We won't push CO2 systems and high-powered lighting on someone setting up their first tank.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we cover</h2>

    <p>
      Our core focus is low-tech planted tanks — setups that grow live plants without CO2 injection,
      complex dosing schedules, or expensive equipment. Within that, we cover betta tanks, shrimp
      and nano setups, beginner plant guides, troubleshooting, and gear recommendations.
    </p>

    <p>
      As the site grows, we'll be expanding into programmatic plant and species guides — detailed
      care pages for hundreds of aquarium plants, fish, and shrimp species, built from structured
      research data.
    </p>

    <h2>Affiliate disclosure</h2>

    <p>
      Some links on this site are affiliate links — we may earn a small commission if you purchase
      through them, at no extra cost to you. This is how we keep the site running and the content
      free. We only recommend products we'd genuinely suggest to a friend setting up their first
      tank. See our full <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a> for details.
    </p>

    <h2>Get in touch</h2>

    <p>
      Questions, corrections, or topic suggestions — we read everything sent to
      <a href="/contact/">our contact page</a>. If something in a guide is wrong or outdated,
      please tell us. Accuracy matters more than being right.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
